                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "An easy but fun Megaman clone that is lacking the tough-as-nails difficulty of the first game while still being totally decent as its own thing. It's basically kinda like how Megaman 2 was much more user friendly than Megaman 1. It's a more polished and less stressful experience, but something gets lost when you make a game too winnable with extra items, powerups, etc. \r\n\r\nThe joy of this game is the different layers to the game play. You have not only your normal attack but special weapons and a Gradius-style overlay that lets you pick powerups as you kill enemies. Beyond this you can also level up your stats, increase your special weapon strength and buy special items. The bosses all have predictable patterns if you practice, and if you buy the healing item you could really just spam attack and win automatically. The level designs are actually much better than the original game. There are a ton of enemy types and the levels have lots of pitfalls and hazards that require practice to get through. \r\n\r\nOnce you beat the game you unlock \"Lunatic\" difficulty and this is actually quite easy if you use the healing item. I made an effort to beat it without the healing items because otherwise the game would just be too easy. Some bosses have weaknesses to weapons but for the most part normal shots will take everything down if you memorize the pattern. I did like that you couldn't just spam attack the bosses with their weaknesses, but many bosses are just way too easy on the normal difficulty and don't really get that much harder on lunatic difficulty. \r\n\r\nThere are three playable characters and technically two different games even though the only real tangible difference is the final boss fight. After beating the game with the other two characters you unlock the bonus character \"Riva\" who was the boss in the first game. Her attacks are much improved but her general game design is very easy. \r\n\r\nThe achievements add a little bit of extra work, but since you can complete most of them on the casual difficulty they can be completed with little resistance. I really like the graphical style, the music is great and the character animations are impressive. The story is nonsense (though in a Megaman game that barely matters) and while I enjoyed the way the combat scales with progression I would've preferred Lunatic mode be a little more restrictive on letting you basically collect enough powerups to win.",
